{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789905459,"nonce":"nNJPs6dWj+1T8Zixo2Cr+4xu6so=","message":"Placed. A scratch copy of the program printing every double as bits shows the world states agreeing exactly at frame 0 and diverging at the first frame with neighbours: for boid 3, `vy + ay * dt * 60` gives ...988 in Wren (game.wren, interpreted or tiered) and ...989 in Haxe, and the exact rational sum rounds to ...989. So Haxe computes it with one rounding: ash forms an FMA for `a * b + c` in every engine, interpreter included (`air_pipeline::fma`, on by default; `--no-fma` clears it), and wren_lift rounds twice except in its top tier, whose `FMAdd` (`codegen/aarch64.rs`) the pure-Wren arena reaches at 1000 x 600 (its checksum then matches Haxe) and not at 1000 x 240 (it matches mixed). The ulp differences feed `acc` and the velocities; the position sum absorbs them, which is why the checksums agree to about 1e-9 relative and no further.\n\nNot a bridge bug: every value crosses bit-exact (`dt`, `n`, the reads and writes all compared equal from the Wren side). The bench now measures the spread and calls a contraction-sized one what it is; a larger one still reads as a different arena.","files":null}]}